From b8139dd23882fa460e3069b78f00b3d4d33c51a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marouene Boubakri <marouene.boubakri@nxp.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Nov 2025 04:12:13 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] IIOTSOL1-956: tls: Enable OpenSSL default and se05x provider
 coexistence
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

In current TLS integration, OpenSSL provider selection was attempted
through explicit property queries such as `provider=nxp_prov` or
`?provider=nxp_prov` when loading the private key or initializing the
TLS context. This approach failed because the SE05x provider does not
include its own PEM or DER decoders; it depends on the default
provider’s generic decoders to parse reference key files before
identifying the embedded SE05x key handle. When a strict property
query was applied, OpenSSL excluded the default provider’s decoders
from the fetch process, so the key could not be parsed at all.

Even with the relaxed `?provider=` form, the resulting key object was
still instantiated by the default provider, meaning that SE05x offload
was never engaged. The correct integration strategy is therefore
to avoid restrictive propqueries entirely and instead control
algorithm precedence by loading the SE05x provider before the default
provider, ensuring that it can claim reference keys once decoding
has completed.

The TLS context initialization was also updated to remove the provider
property query from the `SSL_CTX_new_ex()` call.
Previously, the context was created with a property filter targeting
either the default provider or the custom provider (SE05x provider).
Setting the property to `provider=default` caused all cryptographic
operations, including handshake signing, to be executed by the default
software provider which resulted in invalid or unverifiable signatures
when the private key was a SE05x reference key.

Conversely, setting the property to `provider=nxp_prov` broke the TLS
stack entirely, since the SE05x provider does not implement the full
set of algorithms required for TLS—such as bulk ciphers and message
digests. Using a NULL property query ensures that both providers
remain visible: the default provider continues to supply standard TLS
primitives, while the SE05x provider transparently handles private-key
operations for secure-element keys. This configuration preserves
interoperability for both hardware-backed and software keys without
restricting the algorithm fetch scope.

Consequently, the `custom_key` flag and its associated logic have been
removed from both the TLS context initialization and the private-key
loading paths, since provider selection is now performed implicitly
through the shared context and the defined provider loading order.

In addition, the TLS client code was updated to use `SSL_CTX_new_ex()`
instead of `SSL_CTX_new()`, and to explicitly load the SE05x and default
providers, ensuring that client authentication against the CSMS server
in OCPP deployments correctly uses the private key stored in the SE05x
secure element.

Upstream-Status: Pending

Signed-off-by: Marouene Boubakri <marouene.boubakri@nxp.com>
---
 lib/staging/tls/openssl_util.cpp | 19 -------------------
 lib/staging/tls/tls.cpp          | 25 +++----------------------
 2 files changed, 3 insertions(+), 41 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/staging/tls/openssl_util.cpp b/lib/staging/tls/openssl_util.cpp
index 58f724c24..678df2652 100644
--- a/lib/staging/tls/openssl_util.cpp
+++ b/lib/staging/tls/openssl_util.cpp
@@ -397,22 +397,8 @@ pkey_ptr load_private_key(const char* filename, const char* password) {
      * that causes key file to be opened an additional time
      */
 
-    bool custom_key = false;
-
-    if (filename != nullptr) {
-        fs::path keyfile{std::string(filename)};
-        custom_key = evse_security::is_custom_private_key_file(keyfile);
-    }
-
     OpenSSLProvider provider;
 
-    if (custom_key) {
-        // set global provider to custom settings
-        provider.set_global_mode(OpenSSLProvider::mode_t::custom_provider);
-    } else {
-        provider.set_global_mode(OpenSSLProvider::mode_t::default_provider);
-    }
-
     pkey_ptr private_key{nullptr, nullptr};
     auto* bio = BIO_new_file(filename, "r");
     if (bio != nullptr) {
@@ -425,11 +411,6 @@ pkey_ptr load_private_key(const char* filename, const char* password) {
         BIO_free(bio);
     }
 
-    if (custom_key) {
-        // reset global provider back to default settings
-        provider.set_global_mode(OpenSSLProvider::mode_t::default_provider);
-    }
-
     return private_key;
 }
 
diff --git a/lib/staging/tls/tls.cpp b/lib/staging/tls/tls.cpp
index 818942a56..6ef4be573 100644
--- a/lib/staging/tls/tls.cpp
+++ b/lib/staging/tls/tls.cpp
@@ -421,33 +421,14 @@ bool configure_ssl_ctx(bool is_server, SSL_CTX*& ctx, const char* ciphersuites,
                        const tls::Server::certificate_config_t& cert_config, bool required) {
     bool result{true};
 
-    // TPM2 support is via the OpenSSL provider class OpenSSLProvider in
-    // libevse-security. This needs to be performed before
-    // SSL_CTX_use_PrivateKey_file() or configure_ssl_ctx()  is called
+    OpenSSLProvider provider;
 
     if (is_server) {
-        bool custom_key = false;
-
-        if (cert_config.private_key_file != nullptr) {
-            fs::path keyfile{std::string(cert_config.private_key_file)};
-            custom_key = evse_security::is_custom_private_key_file(keyfile);
-        }
-
-        OpenSSLProvider provider;
-
-        provider.set_global_mode(OpenSSLProvider::mode_t::default_provider);
-
-        if (custom_key) {
-            provider.set_tls_mode(OpenSSLProvider::mode_t::custom_provider);
-        } else {
-            provider.set_tls_mode(OpenSSLProvider::mode_t::default_provider);
-        }
-
         const SSL_METHOD* method = TLS_server_method();
-        ctx = SSL_CTX_new_ex(provider, provider.propquery_tls_str(), method);
+        ctx = SSL_CTX_new_ex(provider, NULL, method);
     } else {
         const SSL_METHOD* method = TLS_client_method();
-        ctx = SSL_CTX_new(method);
+        ctx = SSL_CTX_new_ex(provider, NULL, method);
     }
 
     if (ctx == nullptr) {
